WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.030
What was the question again?

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:04.290
So the question is, like, a positive experience

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.850
we've had with AI, where it's helped enhance

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.070
our productivity learning specifically in the

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:09.450
lab.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:10.620
Cool.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.920
So, I can go first. Yeah, I don't know how many.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:18.980
I would say one of the best ways was,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.750
with the experimentations that we've been running

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.370
on the tower, there's a lot that we didn't understand

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.050
at first that we were kind of just doing to get

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.510
started,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.350
and I helped get started on that.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.850
came a point where I was like, oh,

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.870
I don't really know what's going on with it.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.810
So instead of just asking it what to do next,

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.190
what we did was we asked, like, how does this

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.670
work? What are you doing with this?

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.250
Give us some source papers and stuff is basically

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.610
the thing that I sent to it.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.410
And it provided back a lot of stuff that obviously

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.250
I had to go through in like source check and

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:52.630
stuff.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.870
But the papers and stuff that it provided were

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.550
pretty good because I could go and find these

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.990
papers and see how they relate.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:00.570
Are they good?

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.210
And those provided good information for, like,

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.210
actually learning what was going on and helped

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.130
enhance it in that way.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.510
There's a lot of ways it's enhanced productivity

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.910
with being able to analyze data quickly and stuff

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.230
with things we already know what it's doing.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.410
But, yeah.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:19.140
Nice, nice, nice. This one's pretty good stuff.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.260
For me,

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.760
you know, haven't done anything in this lab quite

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:24.320
yet.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.900
So not much, but I guess an experience in a

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.120
different lab. that I've done.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:33.040
We,

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.960
I had to custom make threads because we had

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.980
a female part, but we

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:43.570
had to make a new male part without ordering

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.630
it online. So I had to learn how to custom make

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.310
the threads. It taught me how to make the threads,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.110
which was pretty good, although it made the diameter

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.670
of the circle too big. So I

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.270
had to make small adjustments, like making it

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.010
the right size.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.200
But it taught me how to make the teeth.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:01.200
pretty well.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.420
That's pretty good. It's funny how bad AI's spatial

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.180
reasoning is.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.340
Like, it really has no concept of, like, 3D space

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.420
at all. I mean, it makes sense because it's just

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:11.980
an algorithm.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:13.280
But,

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.680
yeah. Yeah, like, the way you have it, sure,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.530
yes, it would fit,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.420
but it wouldn't thread. It wouldn't twist.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.300
Yeah, the thrust. Yeah, I could just, you know,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.300
it's in. It's like the funny thing is, like,

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.860
the auger design that they've been doing.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:29.540
I don't know how much of it you've seen, but

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:30.760
it's for, like, a power dispenser,

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.360
and they had AI doing all of the generative CAD

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:33.980
for it.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.680
And at the beginning, it was really funny because

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.600
there'd be all these things, like, great ideas,

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.260
and there'd be, like, parts where just, like,

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.000
it couldn't move because the parts interfered

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:43.880
so much with each other.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:45.720
Well, like, the connector wasn't attached to

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:46.880
the body or things like that.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.920
Like, that AI just doesn't realize.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:49.360
But,

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.980
yeah, I think teaching as, like, that is a really

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.020
good, good thing. Anyway, anything else you want

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:56.660
to add? Nope.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:58.800
Fiss out.
